From aae43d559496b068266298736401ebc27f2911a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 20:07:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07991/11362] [outer-loop] untrack the .venv symlink my own
 previous commit leaked, and close the gitignore hole that let it through

MY DEFECT, caught by reading my own commit's --stat. e871f32be added a
mode-120000 blob at `.venv`: a symlink pointing at the absolute path
/home/ianblenke/github.com/Carnot-EBM/carnot-ebm/.venv. I created it inside the
isolated worktree so the pre-commit torch-CUDA guard could import torch (the
worktree has no venv of its own), then `git add -A` swallowed it.

WHY .gitignore DID NOT STOP IT, which is the reusable lesson. The ignore rule was
`.venv/` -- a TRAILING-SLASH pattern, which git matches against DIRECTORIES ONLY.
A symlink named `.venv` is not a directory, so the pattern never applied. The main
repo has a real `.venv` DIRECTORY, which the rule does cover, so the hole was
invisible until something created a same-named non-directory. Fixed by adding the
slash-less `.venv` and `venv` forms alongside the existing ones, with the incident
recorded inline so the trailing slashes are not "tidied" back later.

WHY IT MATTERS rather than being cosmetic: a committed absolute-path symlink is
machine-specific. On any other clone `.venv` would resolve to a path that does not
exist, and every `.venv/bin/python` pre-commit hook entry in
.pre-commit-config.yaml would fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the
committer's change. This is the same class as the embedded-repo gitlink that broke
CI for 37 minutes.

`git rm --cached .venv` removes ONLY the index entry. The real `.venv` directory in
the working tree is untouched and was verified present after the removal.

Committed via a dedicated `git worktree` for the same reason e871f32be was: two
other writers (the conductor and a peer outer-loop session) are live in the main
working tree, so a pathspec commit there gets its tree stashed out from under the
always-run hooks. This worktree's own `.venv` symlink is deliberately created
AFTER `git rm --cached` and is now ignored by the hardened rule, so it cannot be
re-added.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw
---
 .gitignore | 8 ++++++++
 .venv      | 1 -
 2 files changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 delete mode 120000 .venv

diff --git a/.gitignore b/.gitignore
index 67a6b5736d..630b931892 100644
--- a/.gitignore
+++ b/.gitignore
@@ -18,8 +18,16 @@ build/
 .pytest_cache/
 
 # Virtual environments
+# The trailing-slash forms match DIRECTORIES ONLY. On 2026-07-26 a git worktree
+# was given a `.venv` SYMLINK pointing at the real venv (so the pre-commit
+# torch-CUDA guard could find torch), `git add -A` did NOT ignore it -- a symlink
+# is not a directory, so `.venv/` never matched -- and a machine-specific
+# absolute-path symlink got committed as a mode-120000 blob. The slash-less forms
+# below close that hole for symlinks and plain files of the same name.
 .venv/
+.venv
 venv/
+venv
 env/
 
 # IDE
diff --git a/.venv b/.venv
deleted file mode 120000
index 1ccf8e2d20..0000000000
--- a/.venv
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1 +0,0 @@
-/home/ianblenke/github.com/Carnot-EBM/carnot-ebm/.venv
\ No newline at end of file
-- 
2.55.0

